C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      Example 2.1 from Bishop Et Al Text (https://digitalcommons.usu.edu/ecstatic_all/76/)
      Modifies Example to add a labor constraint
       
      THE PROBLEM:
       
      An irrigated farm can be planted in two crops:  eggplants and tomatoes.  Data are as fol-lows:
       
      Seasonal Resource
      Inputs or Profit        Crops        Resource
      Availability
              Eggplant        Tomatoes
      Water        1x103 gal/plant        2x103 gal/plant      4x106 gal/year
      Land        4 ft2/plant        3 ft2/plant               1.2x104 ft2
      Labor         5hr/plant        2.5/hr plant              17,500 hours
      Profit/plant        $6        $7
       
                      Determine the optimal planting for the two crops.
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 15, 2015
  28   
  29  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  30  SETS time time at the end of the month /June, July, August/
  31       place place the water is being stored /reservoir, spill, diversion/;
  32   
  33  Alias (time,tprev);
  34   
  35  * 2. DEFINE input data
  36  PARAMETERS
  37     Table c(time, place) Objective function constants
  38              reservoir   spill   diversion
  39     June         0         0       150
  40     July         0         0       170
  41     August       0         0       425;
  42   
  43   
  44     Parameters
  45     b(time) Right hand constraint values (per month)
  46      /June    9000,
  47       July    9000,
  48      August   9000/
  49   
  50      i(time) Inflow constraints (per month)
  51      /June    5000,
  52      July     3200,
  53      August   2000/;
  54   
  55    TABLE A(time,place) Left hand side constraint coefficients
  56              reservoir     spill     diversion
  57      June     1              0          0
  58      July     1              0          0
  59      August   1              0          0;
  60   
  61   
  62  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  63  VARIABLES X(time,place) Water released by the end of the month to each place
  64            VPROFIT  total profit ($);
  65   
  66  * Non-negativity constraints
  67  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  68   
  69  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  70  EQUATIONS
  71     PROFIT Total profit ($) and objective function value
  72     MAX_STORAGE(time) Resource Constraints
  73     MIN_STORAGE minimum storage constraint for august
  74     MASS_BALANCE(time);
  75   
  76  PROFIT..                 VPROFIT =E= SUM((time,place), c(time,place)*X(time,place));
  77  MAX_STORAGE(time) ..    SUM(place, A(time,place)*X(time, "reservoir")) =L= b(time);
  78  MIN_STORAGE..           X("August", "reservoir") =G= 3000;
  79  MASS_BAlANCE(time)..         X(time, "reservoir")
  80                             - sum(tprev$(ord(tprev) = ord(time)-1), X(tprev,"reservoir")) - 3000$(ord(time)=1)
  81                             + X(time, "spill")
  82                             + X(time, "diversion")
  83                             =E= i(time);
  84   
  85   
  86  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  87  MODEL RELEASE /PROFIT, MAX_STORAGE, MIN_STORAGE, MASS_BALANCE/;
  88  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  89  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
  90   
  91   
  92  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  93  * Solve the PLANTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  94  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  95  SOLVE RELEASE USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
  96   
  97   
  98  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


